## Authority Table

| Language | Role | Authority ceiling |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sounio | `SEMANTIC_AUTHORITY` | Creates executable semantics and expected results. |
| Lean 4 | `FORMAL_PARITY` | Proves or compares after frozen Sounio semantics. |
| Koka | `EFFECT_PARITY` | Compares effects after frozen Sounio semantics. |
| C++ | `MATERIAL_PARITY` | Measures material/runtime parity; may bootstrap only a declarative transitional Guardian. |
| Haskell | `OPTIONAL_DENOTATIONAL_BASELINE` | Optional denotational comparison after freeze. |
| OCaml | `OPERATIONAL_REALIZATION` | Runs the existing coordinator, journal, recovery, providers, and transitional Guardian after binding the frozen Sounio hash. |
| External LLM | `REVIEW_ONLY` | Reviews; never confirms or promotes results. |
| Python | `PROHIBITED` | Never executable as an oracle or Guardian. |
| Rust | `PROHIBITED` | Never executable as an oracle or Guardian. |

## Evidence Boundary

The Sounio module is the first executable authority, but it is not yet the
system Guardian. Shell scripts only assemble, compile, invoke, and sabotage the
Sounio source. Their comparisons establish process behavior; they do not define
the expected decision table, which remains inside
`loom_language_authority_selftest`.

Until a native pre-action Guardian replaces both Python hook scripts and covers
Exec/Bash, structured writes, commit, and CI, the repository must report this
concept as an executable semantic policy with incomplete enforcement.

